[Black "Introduction to Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Result "*"]

 { I am sure many of you have heard that candidate moves should strictly be calculated in this order: Checks, captures, threats. This is because tactics often arise from these forcing moves. However, there are a few downsides to this method. For one, if the best move happened to be a “quiet” threat, much effort would be wasted calculating irrelevant checks and captures. Even worse, if the best move is defensive, then you are barking up the wrong tree if you consider only checks, captures, and threats. This is why in the previous chapter I recommended that you adopt The Burger Technique: (i) counting the material, (ii) analysing existing/potential threats, and (iii) identifying compelling candidate moves regardless of how forcing they are. For the most part, this approach works well. However, even after completing the first two steps, there could be situations where there are a myriad of candidate moves. The challenge is to find the best candidate move in an efficient way. In this chapter, I will introduce a technique called reciprocal thinking to complement The Burger Technique. This is a technique made popular by Paata Gaprindashvili from his 2004 book ‘Imagination in Chess’. Reciprocal thinking can be implemented after calculating through your first candidate move. If that move fails, do not discard it and randomly hop to another. Instead, diagnose the cause of failure and only consider subsequent candidate moves that rectify the problem. If the new candidate move fixes the current problem but creates another, then look for yet other candidate moves that solve both these problems. Repeat this process until you find a move that gives no problems. As one of the most powerful techniques, reciprocal thinking not only uncovers subtle moves, but also keeps us from examining random moves. To put that into practice, we will be looking through 25 puzzles. For the first three, I will guide you with a few hints on how reciprocal thinking can be integrated into The Burger Technique. Subsequently, you will solve the puzzles unaided and the ideal thought process will be revealed after each one is completed. }  
 *

