 { Step 1: We are down a pawn. Step 2: The plan is clearly to either give checkmate or win material with the discovered attack set up against Black's g7 King. Step 3: Consider candidate moves. One way to try and win the enemy Queen is Ne4+. How do you think Black would try to defend against that? } 1. { -KEY- } Rxf5 { Without first considering 1. Ne4+, the logic behind Rxf5 will sound absolutely ludicrous: The purpose of Rxf5 is to remove the key defender of e6. Let's explore 1. Ne4+ to find out why. } ( 1. Ne4 f6 { Black must create a counterattack on our Queen since his own Queen cannot be saved. } 2. Qc7 { We are not looking for a trade, so our Queen must run with check } Rf7 { Again, Black must block the check with a counterattack on our Queen } 3. Nxg5 ( 3. Qxf7 { We can also try to win the exchange. But since we were down a pawn to begin with, this move isn't enough for a clear victory } Kxf7 4. Nxg5 hxg5 $14 ) 
Rxc7 4. Rxf5 { The idea is 4...exf5 5. Ne6+ where we will be up a piece for a pawn at the end. But why should Black trade his c7 Rook for our f5 Rook here when he can simply win the Knight with } hxg5 { Therefore, Rxf5 must be played much earlier, before all this mess starts } ) 
exf5 ( 1... Qxf5 { loses control over g7. White is easily better after } 2. Ne8 Kg6 3. Qg7 Kh5 4. Nf6 { Black has to give up the Queen with Qxf6 because Kh4 is met with Qg3# } ) 
2. Ne4+ f6 3. Qc7+ Rf7 4. Nxg5 Rxc7 { The exact same line, but Black has already committed to meeting Rxf5 with exf5 } 5. Ne6+ { Now that you have an idea of how to employ reciprocal thinking, the explanations of the rest of the puzzles will only be revealed after they are completed. } 
