1. { -KEY- } a4 { Black's King is exposed, while we have active pieces in an opposite-coloured Bishop ending. This suggests that a king hunt is on the cards. The Black King is planning to flee through b5, and we have to take that square away. The threat is 2. Rc1+ Kd5 3. Rd7+ Kxe5 4. Bc3 } ( 1. Rc1 Kb5 { White may be better, but the advantage is certainly not as big as if the text move were played. } ) 
Rxa4 { The most critical line to consider, since Black has just won the b5 square back. The problem is that the Rook on a4 will suffocate his own King later on. } ( 1... Kd5 { Black cannot run away } 2. Rd7 Kxe5 3. Bc3 ) 
2. Rc1+ ( 2. Rc7 $4 Kb5 { And we cannot play Rb1+ } ) 
Kb5 ( 2... Kd5 3. Rd7 Kxe5 4. Bc3 ) 
