 { To recap, reciprocal thinking requires you to calculate one line, check for its flaw, then find a second candidate move that corrects that flaw. However, there are peculiar instances where the resulting positions are hard to compare. Or there may be too many variations to consider within a single candidate move, let alone two or three. This is why I have created the Superposition Technique which allows us to easily compare two or more candidate moves. The idea is simple: Whenever you have multiple similar candidate moves to consider, calculate all the lines simultaneously, then compare the end positions. Finally, pick the line with the best resulting position. This simple example will illustrate the thought process. } 1. { -KEY- } b5 axb5 { Here we have two moves to consider, 2. a5 and 2. axb5. Let's calculate both lines simultaneously to see which is better. 2. a5/axb5 f4 3. a6/b6 f3 4. a7/b7 f2 5. a8=Q+/b8=Q. Now it is clear that a5 is better since it comes with check and does not give Black time to play f1=Q+. Of course, we could have solved this through other methods, but the purpose is just to illustrate the concept of the Superposition Technique } 2. a5 ( 2. axb5 f4 3. b6 f3 4. b7 f2 5. b8=Q $11 f1=Q ) 
f4 3. a6 f3 4. a7 f2 { Be patient when learning this technique. Although not easy to grasp initially, I can assure you that with conscientious practice, you will develop far greater mental clarity than if you were to rely only on conventional calculation techniques. } 
 *

