1... { -KEY- } Nf5 { White was threatening to play Rg6 to chase the Knight from the defense of g8, and thereafter, play Rg8. The text move harasses the enemy Rook and prepares to displace the enemy monarch from f1, where it blocks our pawn. } 2. Rf7 { If you considered this move before playing 1...Nf5, great job! This is a key variation to consider because it is rather tricky. We cannot take the Rook because of h8=Q+ followed by Kxf2. The only way is to give a Knight check, but which is better? Ng3+ or Ne3+? Is there a difference? Let's calculate them simultaneously with the Superposition Technique. 2...Ng3+/Ne3+ 3. Kxf2 (3. Kg2/Ke2 f1=Q+ 4. Rxf1 Nxf1) 3...Rxf7+ 4.Kxg3/Kxe3 Rxh7 5. Kg4/Kf4. Comparing the two resulting positions, it is now clear that 2...Ne3+ is the better check because we can take on h5 at the end. In contrast, 2...Ng3 allows the King to defend h5 at the end, which will be a nuisance. } Ne3+ ( 2... Ng3 3. Kxf2 Rxf7 4. Kxg3 Rxh7 5. Kg4 ) 
3. Kxf2 Rxf7+ 4. Kxe3 Rxh7 5. Kf4 Rxh5 
 *

