1... { -KEY- } Rd4+ { A brief look at 1...f5 will show that the King easily escapes } ( 1... f5 2. Ke5 Bd4 3. Ke6 ) 
2. Ke3 { Here, our d4 Rook can unleash a myriad of discovered attack via two axes. This calls for the Superposition Technique. Let's start with the horizontal one. If 2...Rf4+/Rg4+, then 3. Kd3 and White is prepared to shield any horizontal checks with 4. Re3 or 4. Ne3. Even winning the Queen for the Rook with 2...Rh4+ is not enough because we are already down almost a whole Queen. If 2...Rxc4+, then 3. Kd3 Rd4+ 4. Kc3 and we are out of checks. Let's now consider Rook checks along the vertical. For obvious reasons, I will not consider 2...Rd3+. 2...Rd1+/Rd2+/Rd5+/Rd6+/Rd7+/Rd8+ 3. Ke4 With White threatening Qh8+, our next move must come with check. In the line 2...Rd5+, the only check that saves our Rook is 3...Rd4+, which repeats the position. Keeping in mind that we have at least a draw, we will omit 2...Rd5+ in our Superposition calculation. Resetting our calculation from move 2, after 2...Rd1+/Rd2+/Rd6+/Rd7+/Rd8+ 3. Ke4, the only good check is 3...f5+. This forces 4. Ke5 where the only good check is 4...Bd4+. Here, if the Rook were on d1 or d2, the King can escape through e6 or even d5. If the Rook were on d7 or d8, the King can escape through e6. But if our Rook were on d6, it would be checkmate. Therefore, 2...Rd6 is the answer. } Rd6+ ( 2... Rh4 3. Kd3 Rxh1 4. Rxh1 $18 ) 
 ( 2... Rg4 3. Kd3 Rg3 4. Ne3 ) 
 ( 2... Rxc4 3. Kd3 Rd4 4. Kc3 ) 
 ( 2... Rd5 3. Ke4 f5 4. Kxd5 ) 
 ( 2... Rd1 3. Ke4 f5 4. Ke5 Bd4 5. Ke6 ) 
 ( 2... Rd8 3. Ke4 f5 4. Ke5 Bd4 5. Ke6 ) 
3. Ke4 f5+ 4. Ke5 Bd4# 
