 { Black is about to create a second Queen. Therefore, our only hope is the h-pawn, and we need to make use of it fast. } 1. { -KEY- } Be5 { Non-Bishop moves are far too slow. Rc6+ can be met with ...Kxh7 while other moves can be met with e1=Q. The question is, which square should we move our Bishop to? Bg7, Bb2, and Ba1 hang the Bishop outright while Bc3 can be met with ...Qxc2+. Hence, we are going to use the Superposition Technique to consider 1. Bf6/Be5/Bd4 all at once. Black is forced to play 1...Kxh7 and we have the powerful 2. Rc8!. Black has no way of stopping Rh8# other than 2...Qxc2+ 3. Rxc2 e1=Q 4. Rh2+ Kg8. In this resulting position, we need to cover the f7 escape square if we want to win the game. Our Rook will be busy guarding the 8th rank when it lands on h8 while our dark-squared Bishop cannot cover light squares. Furthermore, it must remain on the a1-h8 diagonal to protect the h8 square for the Rook. Therefore, it is up to our King to move forward and control f7. When we consider which variation, Bd4, e5, or f6, allows us to advance our King to control f7 safely, we realise that the Bishop is best placed on e5. This is because it allows us to safely play 5. Ke6 to block the f7 square, and we will have Rh8# next. } Kxh7 2. Rc8 Qxc2+ 3. Rxc2 e1=Q 4. Rh2+ Kg8 5. Ke6 
