 { The Superposition Technique can be applied to opponent moves as well. Here, a brief calculation reveals that Black's Queenside pawns are unstoppable. Hence, we must create a counterattack on the King instead. You may notice that our effort to attack the King may be thwarted either by a1=Q or b1=Q, both of which offer Black unique defensive value. It is up to you if you'd like to calculate them separately or simultaneously. } 1. { -KEY- } Ndf4 { This move controls g6 and weaves a mating net around the King. The f4 Knight controls g6 and h5 while the h3 Knight controls g5. 1. Kf7 allows Black to Queen his pawn while his King is free. Now comes the tricky part - We know that we are going to play g6-g7-g8, but which pawn will Black push? Let's use the Superposition Technique to consider both possibilities. 1...a2/b2 2. g6+Kh6 3. g7 a1=Q/b1=Q. Queening our own pawn will result in an unstoppable barrage of checks, so let's consider “Knighting” it with check instead. 4. g8=N+ Kh7 5. Ng5+ Kh8. If his Queen is on a1, then 6. Ng6#. If his Queen is on b1, then 6. Nf7+ Kh7 7. Nf6#. } ( 1. Kf7 b2 2. g6 Kh6 3. Nxb2 axb2 4. g7 b1=Q $11 5. g8=Q $11 ) 
b2 ( 1... a2 2. g6 Kh6 3. g7 a1=Q $11 4. g8=N Kh7 5. Ng5 Kh8 6. Ng6 ) 
2. g6+ Kh6 3. g7 b1=Q 4. g8=N+ Kh7 5. Ng5+ Kh8 6. Nf7+ Kh7 7. Nf6# 
 *

