 { There are positions where the Superposition Technique is ill-suited. The following puzzle is one such example. The motifs and parameters of this puzzle are clear: We are deciding between Kc1 and Kc2 to put our opponent in semi-stalemate. The trick is to make sure that we leave Black with some legal pawn moves on the Kingside while ensuring that he does not promote in time or with a check on g1 or h1. With two similar candidate moves, is the Superposition Technique best applied here? } 1. { -KEY- } Kc1 { Using the Superposition Technique, it may get confusing as to which line originated from which move, since the King will be oscillating between c1 and c2 so much. Instead, we should focus on one line. If it is good, we should certainly go for it. Otherwise, we can use reciprocal thinking to determine if the other move is better. Without further ado, let's start calculating! I have chosen 1. Kc2 to start us off. } ( 1. Kc2 g6 ( 1. Kc1 h6 { Taking on h6 or playing 2. g6 clearly leads to stalemate. We must wait with our King. } 2. Kc1 g6 ( 2. Kc1 hxg5 $4 3. hxg5 g6 4. Kc2 gxh5 5. g6 h4 6. g7 h3 7. g8=Q $11 h2 8. Qg7 { It's worthwhile noting that White wins in this line even if his King were on c1 now instead of c2. } ) 
3. Kc2 ( 3. gxh6 gxh5 ) 
 ( 3. hxg6 h5 ) 
gxh5 ( 3. Kc1 hxg5 $4 4. h6 gxh4 5. h7 h3 6. h8=Q { Again, our King could be on c1 here and we would still win. } ) 
4. Kc1 { Continuing to avoid stalemate } hxg5 5. hxg5 h4 6. g6 h3 7. g7 h2 8. g8=Q $11 h1=Q { Draw. Had we played 1. Kc1, our King would be on c2 now and we will have 9. Qg7# } ) 
2. Kc1 gxh5 ( 2. Kc1 h6 { Transposes to the drawing line } ) 
3. Kc2 h6 4. Kc1 hxg5 5. hxg5 h4 6. g6 h3 7. g7 h2 8. g8=Q $11 h1=Q { Draw. This would, again, have been a win if our King were on c2. To conclude our calculation for 1. Kc2, we only have a draw. Those draws would have been wins if our King were on c2, avoiding a check when Black Queens. In the lines where we do win after 1. Kc2, we had enough extra tempi and would still win even if our King were on the other square. Hence, 1. Kc1 wins the game } ) 
g6 ( 1... h6 2. Kc2 hxg5 3. hxg5 g6 4. Kc1 gxh5 5. g6 h4 6. g7 h3 7. g8=Q $11 h2 8. Qg7 { Just in the nick of time } ) 
2. Kc2 ( 2. hxg6 $4 h5 { It is a draw by stalemate on the next move. } ) 
gxh5 ( 2... h6 3. Kc1 hxg5 ( 3... gxh5 4. Kc2 ) 
4. h6 ) 
3. Kc1 h6 4. Kc2 hxg5 5. hxg5 h4 6. g6 h3 7. g7 h2 8. g8=Q h1=Q 9. Qg7# 
 *
